\description{
Returns the LPJmL per-crop-functional-type (CFT) consumptive blue and green
water and net irrigation requirement \strong{per band}, rather than summed to the
cell as \code{\link[=build_water_balance]{build_water_balance()}} reports them. Each value is the band's
water expressed as a depth over the whole cell (mm/yr): the per-stand
density LPJmL writes, multiplied by the band's stand fraction. That is the
same weighting \code{\link[=build_water_balance]{build_water_balance()}} applies before summing, so summing
this output over bands per cell-year returns its \code{blue_consump_mm},
\code{green_consump_mm} and \code{cft_nir_mm} exactly.

It is the annual, consumptive counterpart of \code{\link[=build_crop_water_use]{build_crop_water_use()}},
which keeps the \emph{applied} irrigation (\code{cft_airrig_month}) per crop and
month. The two read different LPJmL outputs and answer different
questions (water evaporated by a crop versus water delivered to it), and
share the band vocabulary and the \code{crop_group} column.

The crop dimension is LPJmL's, not WHEP's: a run distinguishes about 16
CFTs (each rainfed and irrigated), and many WHEP items share one CFT --
wheat and barley are both \verb{temperate cereals}, and most fruit, vegetable,
fibre and stimulant crops fall into \code{others}. Water is therefore resolved
\strong{per CFT band}, never per WHEP item. Mapping a band to items (for
example through \code{cft_mapping}) and deciding how to split it among the
items that share it are left to the caller; neither is done here.

Two cautions carried over from the reader. On an LPJmL 6.x run without the
green/blue fix (lbm364dl/LPJmL#3) the blue/green split of the consumptive
cubes is unusable; the same data check \code{\link[=build_water_balance]{build_water_balance()}} runs warns
when rainfed bands carry blue water (class \code{whep_rainfed_blue_water}),
while \code{blue + green} stays valid. And \code{cft_nir_mm} is the net irrigation
\emph{requirement}, not the gross water applied; which of the two a footprint
should charge is a methodological choice.
}
